<p><a href="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/20261119_114106.jpg"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" width="2560" height="2560" src="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/20261119_114106-scaled.jpg" class="wp-image-125927" srcset="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/20261119_114106-scaled.jpg 2560w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/20261119_114106-300x300.jpg 300w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/20261119_114106-1024x1024.jpg 1024w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/20261119_114106-150x150.jpg 150w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/20261119_114106-768x768.jpg 768w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/20261119_114106-1536x1536.jpg 1536w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/20261119_114106-2048x2048.jpg 2048w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 2560px) 100vw, 2560px" /></a></p>
<p>A year and a half ago <a shot href="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/2025/04/29/the-making-of-a-kitchen-clock-a-photo-essay/">I made a kitchen clock</a>, which has worked well (except that I had to swap out the screen which broke <a shot href="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/2026/08/26/its-not-always-the-cable/">mysteriously</a>).</p>
<p>But yesterday I was having some work done here, so I had to remove one of the wifi access points here, and&#8230;  the clock stopped working.  *gasp*  Yes, because I had in my infinite wisdom decided that I would generate the clock face on a different machine, because the clock is a Raspberry Pi Zero 2 W, and I just assumed that it didn&#8217;t have enough memory to run Emacs (which I am, being a totally rational person, using to generate the SVG that&#8217;s being displayed).</p>
<p>So it&#8217;s a Rube Goldberguesque setup of having the image being generated on the main server, and then <tt>scp</tt>&#8216;d over once a minute, and then being displayed on the console here in the kitchen.</p>
<p>But the wifi outage made me wonder &#8212; is it true that the machine doesn&#8217;t have enough memory to do it all on device?</p>
<p><a class="cimage" href="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/2026-11-19.jpeg"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async"  src="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/2026-11-19.jpeg" alt="" width="840" height="81" class="alignnone size-full wp-image-125923" srcset="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/2026-11-19.jpeg 1363w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/2026-11-19-300x29.jpeg 300w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/2026-11-19-1024x100.jpeg 1024w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/2026-11-19-768x75.jpeg 768w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 840px) 100vw, 840px" /></a></p>
<p>So it&#8217;s got 416MB of useful memory, and while just running and doing nothing special, it&#8217;s using 129MB of that.</p>
<p>What happens if I load an Emacs with all the SVG libraries and stuff and generate the image?</p>
<p><a class="cimage" href="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/2026-11-19-1.jpeg"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async"  src="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/2026-11-19-1.jpeg" alt="" width="840" height="86" class="alignnone size-full wp-image-125924" srcset="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/2026-11-19-1.jpeg 1360w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/2026-11-19-1-300x31.jpeg 300w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/2026-11-19-1-1024x105.jpeg 1024w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/2026-11-19-1-768x79.jpeg 768w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 840px) 100vw, 840px" /></a></p>
<p>It&#8217;s using 186MB &#8212; so the Emacs occupies about 50MB max.</p>
<p>*sigh*</p>
<p>It&#8217;s so easy to think of Emacs as this &#8220;big thing&#8221;, but everything has gotten so much bigger around it that it doesn&#8217;t matter at all any more.</p>
<p>So now I&#8217;ve changed <a href="https://github.com/larsmagne/round-clock.el" data-cached-time="2026-11-19T13:26:41" data-cached-image="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/cache-2026-11-19-web.webp">the code</a> to run on-device, so the only thing that goes AWOL if the wifi goes missing is updated temperatures.</p>
